## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical fault-tolerance thinking in multi-tenant LLM ops, focusing on isolation, degradation, and testability.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLAs; circuit breakers; graceful degradation
- Fallback models and caching; backoff strategies
- Incident testing with staged outages

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of a simple per-tenant circuit breaker
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(limit=5, timeoutMs=1000){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; this.limit=limit; this.timeoutMs=timeoutMs; }
  recordFailure(){ this.failures++; if(this.failures>=this.limit) this.state='OPEN'; }
  reset(){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; }
  canProceed(){ return this.state!=='OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate outages in a CI pipeline?
- How would you dimension fallbacks for tenants with strict latency budgets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world hybrid edge-cloud serving with data locality, SLA management, and per-tenant isolation under fluctuating network conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality and residency per tenant
- Edge vs cloud routing and burst handling
- Per-tenant model pools and versioning
- Observability, fault tolerance, and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluator
function routeRequest(req, policyStore){
  const p = policyStore.get(req.tenantId);
  if(p.locality === 'edge' && req.latencyBudget >= EDGE_LATENCY){
    return 'edge';
  }
  return 'cloud';
}
```

