## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to translate privacy and safety requirements into a concrete, low-overhead component that precedes LLM calls and manages per-tenant policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy surface and versioning
- Lightweight, rule-based prompt classification
- Redaction of PII and disallowed content
- Caching decisions to minimize latency and repeated processing

## Code Example
```javascript
function classifyPrompt(prompt) {
  const p = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (/ssn|credit card|password/.test(p)) return 'PII';
  if (p.includes('invoice') || p.includes('order')) return 'TRANSACTION';
  return 'GENERAL';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to specify per-tenant containment and safe routing, a core llm-ops skill.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy isolation
- Prompt containment and redaction
- Routing to model variants
- Atomic policy updates and testability

## Code Example
```javascript
function applyPolicy(tenantId, prompt) {
  const policy = policyStore[tenantId];
  if (policy?.blocks?.some(b => prompt.includes(b))) return {blocked: true};
  let redacted = prompt;
  policy?.redacts?.forEach(r => {
    redacted = redacted.replace(new RegExp(r, 'g'), '[REDACTED]');
  });
  const routeModel = policy?.routeModel ?? 'balanced';
  return {blocked: false, redactedPrompt: redacted, routeModel};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions during policy updates?
- How would you extend the policy model to support multi-tenant auditing without leaking tenant data?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design data lineage and prompt hygiene features in a practical, beginner-friendly manner. It evaluates understanding of per-tenant privacy requirements and the ability to translate abstract privacy needs into concrete models and tests without requiring knowledge of large-scale system design.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant provenance tracking (input, output, model version)
- Simple redaction policy for PII detection and handling during ingestion and storage
- Lightweight policy schema and data model design
- Minimal test plan with concrete PII examples
- Data residency considerations and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal data lineage model
type LineageRecord = {
  tenantId: string;
  requestId: string;
  timestamp: Date;
  input: {
    prompt: string;
    redactedPrompt?: string;
    tokens: number;
  };
  output?: {
    response: string;
    redactedResponse?: string;
    tokens: number;
  };
  model: {
    provider: string;
    version: string;
  };
};

// Simple redaction policy
const redactionPolicy = {
  patterns: [
    { type: 'EMAIL', regex: /\b[\w.-]+@[\w.-]+\.\w+\b/g },
    { type: 'PHONE', regex: /\b\d{3}[-.]?\d{3}[-.]?\d{4}\b/g },
    { type: 'SSN', regex: /\b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b/g },
  ],
  replacement: '[REDACTED]',
};

function redactPII(text: string): string {
  let redacted = text;
  for (const { regex } of redactionPolicy.patterns) {
    redacted = redacted.replace(regex, redactionPolicy.replacement);
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world needs balance cost, latency, and reliability across tenants. This asks for a concrete policy language, a routing strategy, and test plans under varying traffic. It also probes drift handling and failover.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant QoS policies
- Multi-model routing with cost/latency prediction
- Data locality and failover strategies
- Observability: audit logs, drift tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing sketch
function route(req, tenant) {
  const p = policy[tenant];
  const candidates = models.filter(m => p.preferredModels.includes(m.name));
  // pick cheapest that meets latency
  return candidates
    .map(m => ({m, est: predict(m, req)}))
    .filter(x => x.est.lat <= p.max_latency)
    .sort((a,b) => a.est.cost - b.est.cost)[0]?.m;
}
```

